Chapter: Fat32Longname

  cites Foreword chapter Fat32
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16

 FAT32 answered every name under its 8.3 spelling until 2026-09-01, and
 `Fat16.codex` "Two scanners over the same bytes" says why: the assembling
 scanner carries run state, a FAT32 directory is walked one 512-byte sector at
 a time through a cluster chain, and pointing the assembling scanner at a walk
 that dropped the state between sectors would have caught a run only when it
 happened to fall inside one sector.

 So the arm that matters here is B, where the run STRADDLES a sector boundary.
 Arm A passes the moment the scanner is swapped at all; only B fails when the
 state is not threaded, and B is the reason this chapter exists.

 The runs are laid down with the format's own primitives rather than through a
 FAT32 writer, because there is no FAT32 long-name writer yet and because a
 reader graded by our own encoder would agree with itself whatever both did.
 The checksum is taken from the short entry's bytes AFTER they are written,
 which is the same quantity `fat16-lfn-final` recomputes when it decides a run
 belongs to the entry beneath it.

 WHAT THIS CHAPTER DOES NOT COVER, so its silence is not read as agreement.
 The state is threaded across two boundaries and this chapter grades one of
 them. Arm B straddles a SECTOR boundary inside one cluster, which is what a
 16 MB volume gives: `fat32-format` picks 8 sectors per cluster, so the root
 directory is 128 slots and slot 16 is the second sector of the same cluster.
 The CLUSTER boundary, threaded by `fat32-find-in-dir-step` passing the state
 to the next walk, is graded by `codex/test/fat32-cluster-lfn`. This paragraph
 used to say that arm could not be written without leaning on a writer this
 chapter avoids, and that was wrong: growing a root directory by hand is two
 `fat32-set-fat-entry` calls and a zeroed cluster, which are FAT primitives
 older than any long-name work, and that chapter grows it that way and lays
 its runs down by hand exactly as this one does. Shrinking the volume does not
 reach the cluster boundary either: at 8 MB the format picks one sector per
 cluster, the root is 16 slots,
 and a run placed at slot 14 runs off the END of a single-cluster directory
 rather than into a second one, which is a broken fixture and not a boundary
 test. Measured 2026-09-01: that arrangement reports arm B absent for that
 reason and would read as a defect.

 We say:

Section: Placing A Run By Hand

 A slot is addressed by absolute byte, so a record that lands in the next
 sector is read and written there without the placer knowing it happened.
 That is the same arithmetic `fat16-write-lfn-slot` uses.

  f32ln-put-lfn : Integer, Text, Integer, Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Block] Integer
  f32ln-put-lfn (at) (name) (seq) (sum) (last) = act
    buf <- block-read-sector (at / 512)
    let p = fat16-poke-lfn-record buf (int-mod at 512) name seq sum last
    in block-write-sector (at / 512) buf
  end

  f32ln-put-short : Integer, Text -> [Device.Block] Integer
  f32ln-put-short (at) (n83) = act
    buf <- block-read-sector (at / 512)
    let p = fat32-write-entry-fields buf (int-mod at 512) n83 32 0 0
    in block-write-sector (at / 512) buf
  end

  f32ln-sum-at : Integer -> [Device.Block] Integer
  f32ln-sum-at (at) = act
    buf <- block-read-sector (at / 512)
    fat16-short-checksum buf (int-mod at 512)
  end

 A deleted marker rather than a real entry, because the scan stops dead at the
 first 0x00 slot and would never reach arm B. 0xE5 keeps the walk going and
 resets the run state, which is also what a real unrelated entry would do.

  f32ln-pad : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Block] Integer
  f32ln-pad (at) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else act
      buf <- block-read-sector ((at + i * 32) / 512)
      let p = poke-byte buf (int-mod (at + i * 32) 512) 229
      in block-write-sector ((at + i * 32) / 512) buf
      f32ln-pad at (i + 1) n
    end

  f32ln-place : Integer, Text, Text -> [Device.Block] Integer
  f32ln-place (base) (long) (n83) =
    let records = fat16-lfn-records-needed (text-length long)
    in act
      s <- f32ln-put-short (base + records * 32) n83
      sum <- f32ln-sum-at (base + records * 32)
      f32ln-run base long sum records 0
    end

  f32ln-run : Integer, Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Block] Integer
  f32ln-run (base) (long) (sum) (records) (j) =
    if j >= records then 0
    else act
      w <- f32ln-put-lfn (base + j * 32) long (records - j) sum (j == 0)
      f32ln-run base long sum records (j + 1)
    end

Section: Reporting

  f32ln-say : Maybe Fat16DirEntry -> Text
  f32ln-say (m) = when m
    is Just (e) -> "found " & (e.de-name)
    is None -> "<none>"

  f32ln-name : Integer, Text -> Text
  f32ln-name (n) (acc) =
    if n <= 0 then acc
    else f32ln-name (n - 1) (acc & "L")

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Device.Block] Nothing
  opening = act
    fmt <- fat32-format 2048 30720 "CODEX"
    print-line-uni ("format " & show fmt)

    vol <- fat32-init 2048
    print-line-uni ("root cluster " & show (vol.f32-root-cluster)
      & " spc " & show (vol.f32-sectors-per-cluster))

    let sec = fat32-cluster-sector vol (vol.f32-root-cluster)
    in let base = sec * 512
    in act
      a <- f32ln-place base "LongNameOnFat32.txt" "LONGNA~1TXT"
      print-line-uni ("A records " & show (fat16-lfn-records-needed (text-length "LongNameOnFat32.txt")))

      p <- f32ln-pad (base + 3 * 32) 0 11

      let bn = f32ln-name 30 ""
      in act
        b <- f32ln-place (base + 14 * 32) bn "SPANNE~1TXT"
        print-line-uni ("B records " & show (fat16-lfn-records-needed 30)
          & " first slot 14 so the run crosses slot 16")

        e1 <- fat32-find-in-dir vol (vol.f32-root-cluster) "LongNameOnFat32.txt"
        print-line-uni ("A long name inside one sector " & f32ln-say e1)

        e2 <- fat32-find-in-dir vol (vol.f32-root-cluster) bn
        print-line-uni ("B run straddling a sector " & f32ln-say e2)

        e3 <- fat32-find-in-dir vol (vol.f32-root-cluster) "LONGNA~1.TXT"
        print-line-uni ("A still answers its alias " & f32ln-say e3)

        e4 <- fat32-find-in-dir vol (vol.f32-root-cluster) "LongNameOnFat32"
        print-line-uni ("no truncated ghost " & f32ln-say e4)

        e5 <- fat32-find-in-dir vol (vol.f32-root-cluster) "NoSuchNameHere.txt"
        print-line-uni ("absent name " & f32ln-say e5)
      end
    end
  end

